        "title": "4. Step-by-Step Protocol Execution & Production Engineering",
        "content": "When **1.3 The Network Core: Forwarding, Routing, Packet Switching vs. Circuit Switching** executes in a live production network — whether inside an operating system kernel's network stack, an ISP core router ASIC, or a cloud data center fabric — packet processing follows a strict four-phase operational lifecycle:\n\n1. **Phase 1 — State Initialization & Configuration:** Protocol entities initialize local state variables, socket descriptors, cryptographic keys, or routing/forwarding table entries for **Store-and-forward packet transmission**.\n2. **Phase 2 — Header Encapsulation & Message Exchange:** Outbound messages or packets are constructed with explicit protocol header fields (`Seq`, `ACK`, `TTL`, `CIDR prefix`, or `LSA/BGP attributes`) to coordinate **output buffer queueing**.\n3. **Phase 3 — Algorithmic Evaluation & State Transition:** Upon receiving a frame, segment, or control-plane update, the node executes its deterministic update rule (such as longest-prefix matching, EWMA RTT calculation, or shortest-path relaxation) to handle **FDM/TDM circuit switching**.\n4. **Phase 4 — Fault Recovery, Convergence & Verification:** Timers, checksums, duplicate ACK thresholds, or control-plane keepalives detect anomalies and converge the network to a consistent state across **statistical multiplexing efficiency**.\n\n> **Production Engineering Insight:** When debugging **1.3 The Network Core: Forwarding, Routing, Packet Switching vs. Circuit Switching** in Wireshark or `tcpdump`, always correlate wire-level header fields with the endpoint or router state transitions simulated in the Python script below."
