        "content": "![2.2 The Web & HTTP/1.1: Non-Persistent vs. Persistent Connections, Messages & Status Codes Architecture](/images/networking/tcp-ip-five-layer-stack.svg)\n\nIn **Kurose & Ross (*Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach, 9th Edition*, Chapter 2)**, **2.2 The Web & HTTP/1.1: Non-Persistent vs. Persistent Connections, Messages & Status Codes** forms a foundational pillar of **Application Layer Protocols (HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, HTTP/3, DNS, SMTP, DASH)**. Modern packet-switched networks must coordinate distributed protocol state machines across heterogeneous links while maintaining high throughput, predictable latency, and strict interoperability. Specifically, this lesson examines: HTTP client-server transactions over TCP, 2*RTT non-persistent overhead vs. persistent pipelining, ASCII request/response header syntax, and status codes (200, 301, 400, 404).\n\nFrom an architectural standpoint, networking protocols in Chapter 2 decouple high-level service abstractions from underlying hardware constraints. Across the topics covered in this module — including **Web and HTTP, HTTP overview, HTTP connections: two types, Non-persistent HTTP: example, Non-persistent HTTP: response time** — each protocol entity exchanges well-defined header fields, maintains local state variables or forwarding tables, and reacts deterministically to packet arrivals, timers, and control-plane updates.\n\n- **Core Architectural Scope:** Establishes the formal rules, message formats, and state transitions governing HTTP client-server transactions over TCP.\n- **Protocol State & Header Coordination:** Synchronizes distributed endpoints and intermediate packet switches through 2*RTT non-persistent overhead vs. persistent pipelining.\n- **Performance & Scalability Mechanics:** Optimizes bandwidth utilization, convergence time, and queueing behavior via ASCII request/response header syntax.\n- **Production Internet Integration:** Operates seamlessly within the 5-layer TCP/IP protocol stack alongside status codes (200, 301, 400, 404).",
