        "title": "2. Formal Model, Equations & Protocol Specification Table",
        "content": "To analyze **2.2 The Web & HTTP/1.1: Non-Persistent vs. Persistent Connections, Messages & Status Codes** quantitatively, computer networking relies on precise mathematical models, bit-level header specifications, and deterministic state-update equations. The Governing Equation and Architectural Reference Table below summarize the core relationships for **Chapter 2 (Application Layer Protocols (HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, HTTP/3, DNS, SMTP, DASH))**:\n\n$$T_{\\text{HTTP/1.1 Non-Persistent}} = 2 \\cdot \\text{RTT} + \\frac{L_{\\text{object}}}{R}, \\qquad \\Delta_{\\text{queue}} = \\frac{\\Delta_{\\text{LAN}}}{1 - \\beta \\cdot \\Delta_{\\text{LAN}}}$$\n\n| Application Protocol | Transport & Port | Key Architectural Mechanism & Wire Behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **HTTP/1.1 (RFC 9112)** | TCP Port 80 / 443 | Persistent connections with ASCII headers; suffers from TCP & HTTP Head-of-Line (HOL) blocking |\n| **HTTP/2 (RFC 9113)** | TCP Port 443 (TLS) | Binary framing layer, HPACK header compression, interleaved stream frames over a single TCP connection |\n| **HTTP/3 (RFC 9114)** | QUIC over UDP 443 | Independent per-stream reliable delivery and 0-RTT/1-RTT integrated TLS 1.3; eliminates transport HOL blocking |\n| **DNS (RFC 1035)** | UDP/TCP Port 53 | Hierarchical distributed database (Root $\\to$ TLD $\\to$ Authoritative) mapping hostnames to IP records (`A`, `AAAA`, `NS`, `CNAME`, `MX`) |\n| **SMTP / IMAP** | TCP Ports 25, 587 / 143, 993 | Push protocol (`SMTP`) for MTA-to-MTA mail relay; pull protocol (`IMAP`) for client mailbox state synchronization |\n| **DASH & CDNs** | HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 | Client-driven adaptive bitrate video chunk fetching served from Enter-Deep (Akamai) or Bring-Home (Limelight/Netflix) CDN nodes |"
