        "content": "In the official **Kurose & Ross 9th Edition Lecture Slides (Slides 2-18..31)**, the progression of concepts moves systematically from problem formulation to concrete Internet protocol design. Key lecture subtopics in this sequence include **Web and HTTP, HTTP overview, HTTP connections: two types, Non-persistent HTTP: example, Non-persistent HTTP: response time**, illustrating how theoretical networking principles translate directly into deployed IETF RFCs and IEEE standards.\n\nThe following synthesized lecture takeaways capture the essential protocol rules and design trade-offs from the slides:\n\n- **Web and HTTP:** Web page consists of objects, each of which can be stored on different Web servers.\n- **Web and HTTP:** Object can be HTML file, JPEG image, Java applet, audio file,….\n- **Web and HTTP:** Web page consists of base HTML-file which includes several referenced objects, each addressable by a URL, e.g.,.\n- **Client/server model:** Client: browser that requests, receives, (using HTTP protocol) and “displays” Web objects; server: Web server sends (using HTTP protocol) objects in response to requests.\n- **HTTP overview:** Client initiates TCP connection (creates socket) to server, port 80.\n- **HTTP overview:** HTTP messages (application-layer protocol messages) exchanged between browser (HTTP client) and Web server (HTTP server)\n- **HTTP overview:** Server maintains no information about past client requests.\n- **HTTP overview:** If server/client crashes, their views of “state” may be inconsistent, must be reconciled.\n- **HTTP connections: two types:** At most one object sent over TCP connection.\n- **HTTP connections: two types:** Multiple objects can be sent over single TCP connection between client, and that server."
