        "title": "3. Kurose & Ross 9e Lecture Synthesis & Protocol Mechanics (Slides 2-49..58)",
        "content": "In the official **Kurose & Ross 9th Edition Lecture Slides (Slides 2-49..58)**, the progression of concepts moves systematically from problem formulation to concrete Internet protocol design. Key lecture subtopics in this sequence include **HTTP/2: mitigating HOL blocking, HTTP/2 to HTTP/3, QUIC: Quick UDP Internet Connections, QUIC: Connection establishment, QUIC: 0-RTT Connection establishment**, illustrating how theoretical networking principles translate directly into deployed IETF RFCs and IEEE standards.\n\nThe following synthesized lecture takeaways capture the essential protocol rules and design trade-offs from the slides:\n\n- **Key goal:** Decreased delay in multi-object HTTP requests.\n- **HTTP1.1:** Introduced multiple, pipelined GETs over single TCP connection.\n- **Server responds in-order (FCFS:** First-come-first-served scheduling) to GET requests.\n- **Chapter 2 Concept:** With FCFS, small object may have to wait for transmission (head-of-line (HOL) blocking) behind large object(s)\n- **Chapter 2 Concept:** Loss recovery (retransmitting lost TCP segments) stalls object transmission.\n- **Chapter 2 Concept:** Methods, status codes, most header fields unchanged from HTTP 1.1.\n- **Chapter 2 Concept:** Transmission order of requested objects based on client-specified object priority (not necessarily FCFS)\n- **Chapter 2 Concept:** Divide objects into frames, schedule frames to mitigate HOL blocking.\n- **HTTP 1.1:** Client requests 1 large object (e.g., video file) and 3 smaller objects.\n- **Objects delivered in order requested:** O2, O3, O4 wait behind O1."
