        "content": "![4.2 Inside a Router: Input Port Functions, Destination-Based Forwarding & Longest Prefix Matching Architecture](/images/networking/sdn-data-control-plane.svg)\n\nIn **Kurose & Ross (*Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach, 9th Edition*, Chapter 4)**, **4.2 Inside a Router: Input Port Functions, Destination-Based Forwarding & Longest Prefix Matching** forms a foundational pillar of **Network Layer Data Plane (Router Architecture, IPv4/IPv6, CIDR, NAT & OpenFlow)**. Modern packet-switched networks must coordinate distributed protocol state machines across heterogeneous links while maintaining high throughput, predictable latency, and strict interoperability. Specifically, this lesson examines: High-speed router architecture: line termination, link-layer decapsulation, decentralized TCAM forwarding tables, and resolving overlapping prefix rules via Longest Prefix Matching (LPM).\n\nFrom an architectural standpoint, networking protocols in Chapter 4 decouple high-level service abstractions from underlying hardware constraints. Across the topics covered in this module — including **Router architecture overview, Input port functions, Destination-based forwarding, Longest prefix matching** — each protocol entity exchanges well-defined header fields, maintains local state variables or forwarding tables, and reacts deterministically to packet arrivals, timers, and control-plane updates.\n\n- **Core Architectural Scope:** Establishes the formal rules, message formats, and state transitions governing High-speed router architecture: line termination.\n- **Protocol State & Header Coordination:** Synchronizes distributed endpoints and intermediate packet switches through link-layer decapsulation.\n- **Performance & Scalability Mechanics:** Optimizes bandwidth utilization, convergence time, and queueing behavior via decentralized TCAM forwarding tables.\n- **Production Internet Integration:** Operates seamlessly within the 5-layer TCP/IP protocol stack alongside resolving overlapping prefix rules via Longest Prefix Matching (LPM).",
